   "source": [
    "## Homework 2 - Phase I & II Analysis Using CUSUM and EWMA Control Charts\n",
    "### Dr. Austin Brown\n",
    "### Kennesaw State University\n",
    "\n",
    "**DUE: October 10, 2026 via D2L**\n",
    "\n",
    "## Instructions: \n",
    "\n",
    "Buster's Sports Bar & Grill runs a busy draft beer program, pouring a house lager through a long-draw, CO2-driven tap system. State regulations require that a pint be poured within a fairly tight volume tolerance, and management has its own reasons to care about consistency too: an overpour on every pint adds up to real lost profit over a month, while a noticeable underpour draws customer complaints and bad reviews. The general manager has asked your quality control team to investigate the pour consistency at the bar's main tap station.\n",
    "\n",
    "Pours are measured directly from the tap using a calibrated in-line flow meter, in ounces. The general manager has flagged a specific concern: as a CO2 regulator ages, line pressure can creep downward very gradually over weeks or months, producing more foam and a slightly smaller liquid pour -- a small, **sustained** drift, rather than a sudden jump. A chart that's only good at catching a sudden shift could easily miss this kind of slow creep until it's been happening for a long time. Pour volume is treated as a continuous, normally-distributed quality characteristic for this assignment.\n",
    "\n",
    "Your team pulls a baseline (Phase I) sample of pour data from the tap right after a fresh regulator was installed: 5 pints sampled at random each shift, for 25 consecutive shifts. After validating statistical control, the team designs an appropriate CUSUM and EWMA monitoring scheme and applies it to 20 additional shifts of data (Phase II), collected several months later, to determine whether the aging regulator is already producing a detectable drift.\n",
    "\n",
    "## Key Specifications\n",
    "\n",
    "| Quantity | Value |\n",
    "|---|---|\n",
    "| Target pour volume | 16.00 oz |\n",
    "| Lower Specification Limit (LSL) | 15.80 oz |\n",
    "| Upper Specification Limit (USL) | 16.20 oz |\n",
    "| Subgroup size (n) | 5 pints per shift |\n",
    "| Phase I subgroups (m) | 25 shifts |\n",
    "| Phase II subgroups (m) | 20 shifts |"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "db24f175",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Define & Measure\n",
    "\n",
    "### Q1: Briefly describe the purpose of this analysis\n",
    "\n",The analysis aims to determine if the pour volume at Buster's main tap consistently meets the target of 16.00 oz, considering a lower specification limit (LSL) of 15.80 oz and an upper specification limit (USL) of 16.20 oz. 
    "### Q2: Identify the quality characteristic your team will monitor, and classify its units and measurement scale.\n",
    "\n",The quality characteristic of house lager is defined by the liquid volume poured from the tap. This quantitative characteristic operates on a ratio scale. It is not binary data, and is monitored by calculating the means and ranges of five pours per shift.
    "### Q3: Briefly describe why a CUSUM and EWMA chart would be appropriate in this scenario, given the general manager's specific concern about the aging CO2 regulator. What are the limitations of these charts?\n",
    "\n",The manager is concerned about a small, gradual decrease in pour volume over time. A Shewhart chart looks at each shift separately, so it may miss small changes. CUSUM and EWMA use information from previous shifts, making them better at detecting slow, ongoing changes. In this data, the Shewhart chart showed no signals, but both CUSUM and EWMA detected a shift. However, these charts depend on good Phase I estimates, assume normal and independent data, and are less effective for large, sudden changes.
    "## Analyze\n",
    "\n",
    "### Q4: Read the Phase I data into R. Calculate and report the subgroup means and ranges, and use them to estimate the process mean and process standard deviation."
   ]Subgroup means and ranges (all 25 shifts, n = 5):Using all 25 subgroups: 
 x= 16.0386 oz, 
r = 0.1795 oz, so 
o^2 = 0.0772 oz. These are preliminary: they are inflated by the assignable causes
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "1dfbfd33",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "## Q4 Code ##"
   ]
  },library(readxl)

phase1 <- read_excel("BustersSportsBar_PhaseI.xlsx")
pours  <- as.matrix(phase1[, paste0("Pour", 1:5)])
n <- ncol(pours)   # 5 pints per shift
m <- nrow(pours)   # 25 shifts

xbar <- rowMeans(pours)                                  # subgroup means
R    <- apply(pours, 1, function(x) max(x) - min(x))     # subgroup ranges

q4_table <- data.frame(Shift = phase1$Shift, Xbar = round(xbar, 4), R = round(R, 4))
print(q4_table, row.names = FALSE)

# Control chart constants for n = 5
d2 <- 2.326; D3 <- 0; D4 <- 2.114; A2 <- 0.577

xbarbar   <- mean(xbar)       # estimate of process mean
Rbar      <- mean(R)
sigma_hat <- Rbar / d2        # estimate of process standard deviation

cat(sprintf("Grand mean  = %.4f oz\n", xbarbar))
cat(sprintf("Rbar        = %.4f oz\n", Rbar))
cat(sprintf("sigma_hat   = Rbar/d2 = %.4f oz\n", sigma_hat))
